// Basic's environment: the value of a variable of the environment the
// program was started with and a command run through the system's shell,
// each through C's stdlib (libc.rig), a command run with its standard
// output captured, through the system's popen (libc.rig's per-system
// part), and the command line the program was started with, which Preload
// saves before main's first line.
//
// A variable's name and a command go to C as copies ended by a zero
// (to_c_string), which C reads up to its first zero. So a name or a
// command with a zero byte in it would reach C cut short, another name or
// another command, and get_environment, run_command and
// run_command_with_output refuse it without calling C. The copies come
// from the allocator each procedure is given, the context's unless another
// is, and go back to it before the procedure returns, once C is done with
// them.
//
// The systems differ in what they hand C, and Basic doesn't hide it:
// Windows' variable names are case-insensitive, so "Path" finds PATH
// there, and Linux's aren't; a command runs in cmd.exe on Windows and in
// sh on Linux, with each one's syntax, cmd's echo ending its line with
// \r\n and sh's with \n; and its status is the command's exit code on
// Windows, and a wait status on Linux (run_command).
//
// A captured output is the bytes the command wrote to its standard output,
// as it wrote them: on Windows the pipe is read in binary mode, so cmd's
// \r\n stays \r\n, as Basic's files keep their bytes (file.rig), and
// Linux's pipes have no other mode. Only standard output is captured: the
// command's standard error and standard input are the program's own, so
// its errors still reach the program's standard error (a command can send
// them into the pipe too with 2>&1).
//
// A name, a value and a command are bytes to C. Linux's C library takes
// them as they are, so UTF-8 stays UTF-8, but Windows' reads and gives them
// in the system's ANSI code page rather than as UTF-8, so one with a
// character outside ASCII means something else there. Windows'
// wide-character calls, such as _wgetenv, _wsystem and _wpopen, would take
// them right, but Basic doesn't call them yet (issue #209).
#load "libc.rig";
#load "string.rig";
#load "allocators.rig";
#load "memory.rig";
#load "builder.rig";

// A copy of the value of the environment's variable name, in memory from
// allocator, the context's unless given, which the caller gives back with
// free(value.data, allocator), and true. C's getenv gives a pointer to its
// own copy, which a later call may change, so the value is copied out at
// once, its zero not included. A variable set to "" is the zeroed string
// (count 0, data null), which allocates nothing, and true.
//
// It is the zeroed string and false, with nothing left allocated, when the
// variable isn't set, when the allocator has no memory for the name's C
// copy or the value's, and for a name C would read wrong: an empty name,
// one with '=', which ends a name in the environment (NAME=value), and one
// with a zero byte, which C would read as a shorter name.
//
// The name's C copy and the value's are asked for, and the C copy given
// back, with loc, the place of the call unless given (#caller_location,
// spec 7.2), so an allocator that records where it is asked from sees the
// caller's line (allocators.rig).
get_environment :: (name: string, allocator: Allocator = context.allocator, loc: Source_Code_Location = #caller_location) -> string, bool {
    empty : string;
    if name.count == 0 || find_byte(name, "="[0]) >= 0 || find_byte(name, 0) >= 0 { return empty, false; }
    c_name := to_c_string(name, allocator, loc);
    if c_name == null { return empty, false; }
    c_value := c_getenv(c_name);
    free(c_name, allocator, loc);
    if c_value == null { return empty, false; }

    // C's string ends at its first zero, which is how its length is found.
    count := 0;
    while c_value[count] != 0 { count += 1; }
    if count == 0 { return empty, true; }
    data := cast(*u8) alloc(count, allocator, loc);
    if data == null { return empty, false; }
    copy_memory(data, c_value, count);

    value : string;
    value.data = data;
    value.count = count;
    return value, true;
}

// Runs command through the system's shell, cmd.exe on Windows and sh on
// Linux, waits for it to end, and gives its status as C's system gives
// it. On Windows that is the command's exit code: "exit 3" gives 3, and a
// command cmd.exe can't find gives 1. On Linux it is a wait status: the
// exit code times 256 when the command ended by itself ("exit 3" gives
// 768), and 32512 (127 times 256) when sh can't find the command. Either
// way 0 means it succeeded. An empty command, "" or the zeroed string,
// runs the shell with nothing to do, which gives 0: it reaches C as a lone
// zero (to_c_string), never as the null that asks whether there is a
// shell.
//
// It is -1, and nothing runs, for a command with a zero byte, which C
// would read as a shorter command, and when allocator, the context's
// unless given, has no memory for the command's C copy. -1 is also what
// C's system gives when it can't start the shell, and on Windows what a
// command that exits with -1 gives ("exit -1"), so -1 alone doesn't say
// the command never ran.
//
// The program's output may still sit in C's buffer for stdout, where
// Basic's print puts it, while the command writes to the same place
// straight away; so every C stream is flushed first, and what the program
// printed before the command comes out before what the command prints.
//
// The command's C copy is asked for and given back with loc, the place of
// the call unless given, as get_environment's are.
run_command :: (command: string, allocator: Allocator = context.allocator, loc: Source_Code_Location = #caller_location) -> int {
    if find_byte(command, 0) >= 0 { return -1; }
    c_command := to_c_string(command, allocator, loc);
    if c_command == null { return -1; }
    c_fflush(null);
    status := c_system(c_command);
    free(c_command, allocator, loc);
    return status;
}

// Runs command through the system's shell, as run_command does, waits for
// it to end, and gives what it wrote to its standard output, in memory
// from allocator, the context's unless given, which the caller gives back
// with free(output.data, allocator), and its status, as run_command gives
// it: the exit code on Windows, a wait status on Linux, 0 for success on
// both. The output is its bytes as the command wrote them, cmd's \r\n
// included (the header says why). A command that writes nothing gives the
// zeroed string (count 0, data null), which allocates nothing.
//
// Only standard output is captured: what the command writes to standard
// error goes to the program's, as run_command's does, and it reads the
// program's standard input. What the program printed before it is flushed
// first, as run_command flushes it, so it comes out before anything the
// command writes to standard error.
//
// It is the zeroed string and -1, and nothing runs, for a command with a
// zero byte and when allocator has no memory for the command's C copy, as
// run_command's -1 is; and the zeroed string and -1 when C can't start the
// shell. When allocator has no memory for the output, the command has run
// all the same, so its output is lost but its status is still given, with
// the zeroed string.
//
// The output is read from the pipe's file descriptor (c_read), 4096 bytes
// at a time into a buffer on the stack, until a read gives nothing more,
// and gathered in a builder on the heap, whose memory is given back before
// it returns; then it is copied into one allocation of its exact size from
// allocator. The heap is used for the gathering rather than allocator,
// which may be an arena's, where a builder's growing would leave each
// smaller copy behind. Like every builder of Basic's, the heap's stops the
// program when it has no memory left (builder.rig).
//
// The command's C copy and the output are asked for, and the C copy given
// back, with loc, the place of the call unless given, as get_environment's
// are.
run_command_with_output :: (command: string, allocator: Allocator = context.allocator, loc: Source_Code_Location = #caller_location) -> string, int {
    empty : string;
    if find_byte(command, 0) >= 0 { return empty, -1; }
    c_command := to_c_string(command, allocator, loc);
    if c_command == null { return empty, -1; }
    defer free(c_command, allocator, loc);

    c_fflush(null);
    pipe : *void;
    #if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
        // Binary, so cmd's \r\n isn't turned into \n, nor a Ctrl-Z taken
        // for the end.
        pipe = c_popen(c_command, "rb");
    } else {
        // glibc refuses "rb", and its pipes are binary anyway.
        pipe = c_popen(c_command, "r");
    }
    if pipe == null { return empty, -1; }

    gathered : String_Builder;
    builder_init(*gathered, HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
    defer builder_free(*gathered);
    descriptor := c_fileno(pipe);
    buffer : [4096]u8;
    while true {
        // c_read's count and result are 32 bits on Windows and 64 on
        // Linux; 4096 fits either, and so does what it gives.
        got := cast(int) c_read(descriptor, *buffer[0], 4096);
        // On Linux a read a signal stopped before it read anything is
        // made again: it isn't the end.
        #if OS != OS_WINDOWS {
            if got < 0 && c_errno_location().* == C_EINTR { continue; }
        }
        if got <= 0 { break; }
        piece : string;
        piece.data = *buffer[0];
        piece.count = got;
        append_string(*gathered, piece);
    }
    status := cast(int) c_pclose(pipe);

    if gathered.count == 0 { return empty, status; }
    data := cast(*u8) alloc(gathered.count, allocator, loc);
    if data == null { return empty, status; }
    copy_memory(data, gathered.data, gathered.count);

    output : string;
    output.data = data;
    output.count = gathered.count;
    return output, status;
}

// The program's command-line arguments (spec 7.4): the name it was started
// by first, then each argument. On Windows they are UTF-8: a compiled
// program's are converted by Preload from the C runtime's wide arguments,
// an interpreted one's by interpret mode from the wide command line, so
// unlike a variable's value above they don't depend on the ANSI code page;
// on Linux they are the bytes the system gave, as they are. The view is
// empty, not even a name, when the program was started with no argv[0]
// (argc 0, which Linux before 5.18 allowed), so check its count before
// reading the name. Each string is followed by a zero byte its count
// doesn't include, so its data can go to C as it is. main's prologue
// saves them, through Preload's __save_command_line, before main's first
// line, and this gives the view it saved: the same one every call, its
// elements and their bytes the program's for the whole run, never freed,
// and as changeable as any global's. A #run sees the empty view, not even
// a name: no startup runs while compiling (spec 7.8), and the compiler's
// own arguments aren't the program's.
get_command_line_arguments :: () -> []string {
    return __command_line_arguments;
}
